# Copy to web/.env.local for a non-default local setup. Neither variable is
# needed to run `npm run dev` against the standard stack.
#
# ## Both of these end up in the JavaScript bundle
#
# Vite inlines every VITE_-prefixed variable at BUILD time. They are not secrets
# and cannot be made into secrets by any arrangement of this file: anyone who
# opens devtools on the deployed site can read both. That is fine for the API
# URL, which is a public endpoint, and it is the reason the token below is
# described as a speed bump rather than as security.

# --- Where the API is --------------------------------------------------------
# Empty means root-relative `/api/v1`, which is what the Vite dev server proxies
# (see vite.config.js). That is the development setup and needs no value here.
#
# A deployment sets this to the API's own origin - a bare origin, no trailing
# slash, no path:
#
#   VITE_API_BASE_URL=https://cv-screening-api.onrender.com
#
# Without it, a deployed frontend asks its OWN origin for /api/v1/config, the
# host answers with its 404 page, and the client reports "the server answered
# 404 with no message". No CORS setting fixes that - the request never left the
# frontend's origin.
VITE_API_BASE_URL=

# --- The spend guard ---------------------------------------------------------
# Must match UPLOAD_ACCESS_TOKEN on the API. Empty means the client sends no
# header, which is correct when the API's guard is also off.
#
# Sent only on the three endpoints that spend API credit: the two uploads and
# retry. Deliberately not on the dashboard's three-second poll, where a custom
# header would add a CORS preflight to every request for nothing.
VITE_UPLOAD_TOKEN=
